    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "cdb8ecc0",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cdb8ecc0"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Examining the distribution\n",
        "\n",
        "The 90th percentile earns less than half of what the top percentile does: if your household takes home over £800 a week, you're in the top 10%. Let's plot the whole curve:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 52,
      "id": "94ccdd02",
      "metadata": {
        "execution": {
          "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.693924Z",
          "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.693865Z",
          "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.755221Z",
          "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.754823Z"
        },
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 487
        },
        "id": "94ccdd02",
        "outputId": "9cf0d490-6582-456c-d5ef-afb19b1b106e"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 1000x500 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "income[weekly].plot()\n",
        "plt.title(\"UK net equivalised household income by percentile, 2010-11\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Income percentile\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Income per week (GBP)\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "84bd0520",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "84bd0520"
      },
      "source": [
        "The curve is roughly linear in the middle, but rises steeply at the top.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Exercise: means\n",
        "\n",
        "Where does the mean appear here? Draw a horizontal line for the **mean** with `plt.axhline`, and one for the **median** on the same graph. Which is higher, and why? What does the median mean in this context?\n",
        "\n",
        "Hint: last week we used `axvline` to draw vertical lines; `axhline` draws horizontal ones."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 53,
      "id": "e5a96de1",
      "metadata": {
        "execution": {
          "iopub.execute_input": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.756526Z",
          "iopub.status.busy": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.756446Z",
          "iopub.status.idle": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.762542Z",
          "shell.execute_reply": "2026-10-16T10:39:33.759702Z"
        },
        "colab": {
          "base_uri": "https://localhost:8080/",
          "height": 487
        },
        "id": "e5a96de1",
        "outputId": "b2aea2e7-29b5-413e-c2e9-76480148e7e9"
      },
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "display_data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 1000x500 with 1 Axes>"
            ],
            "image/png": "\n"
          },
          "metadata": {}
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "from numpy import percentile\n",
        "# copy and pasted from before (got rid of plt.show())\n",
        "income[weekly].plot()\n",
        "plt.title(\"UK net equivalised household income by percentile, 2010-11\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Income percentile\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Income per week (GBP)\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# to show the mean\n",
        "mean_income = income[weekly].mean()\n",
        "plt.axhline(mean_income, color=\"red\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Mean\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# to show the median\n",
        "median_income = income[weekly].median()\n",
        "plt.axhline(median_income, color=\"blue\", linestyle=\"--\", label=\"Median\")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.show() # can't get labels to show for some reason\n",
        "\n",
        "# the median < mean indicating positive/right skew\n",
        "#(higher data values on the right due to exponential growth at high percentiles)\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "income[weekly].plot()\n",
        "# plt.axhline(income[weekly].mean(), )\n",
        "\n",
        "mean = income[weekly].mean()\n",
        "print(mean)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.axhline(mean, color=\"red\", linestyle=\"--\", label=f\"Mean: £{mean:.0f}\") # passing it an f str - insert curly braces\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
